  "summary": "Painted rooms, walking characters, and verbs — the coziest genre of game art.",
  "description": "Point-and-click adventure art is the illustrated background tradition of Sierra and LucasArts: every screen a painting (first 256-color, then hi-res) with strong foreground props, readable inventory items and characters composited in sprite or rotated-3D. The style ranges from King's Quest storybook watercolor to Monkey Island's stylized caricature and Full Throttle's film-noir shading; Tim Schafer-era backgrounds hold up as standalone illustration. Modern revivals (Thimbleweed Park, Return to Monkey Island, Broken Sword remasters) keep the grammar — hand-painted rooms, verb rows, elegant cursor puzzles — alive as comfort gaming's visual dialect.",
  "culturalContext": "Point-and-click backgrounds are comfort gaming's illustrated canon: LucasArts and Sierra's painted rooms trained a generation's visual taste, Tim Schafer-era plates hang as standalone illustration, and the revival scene (Thimbleweed Park, Return to Monkey Island) keeps hand-painted rooms and verb rows a living dialect.",
